          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Goneril\",\"addressee\":\"Lear\",\"anchor_quote\":\"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\\nDearer than eyesight, space, and liberty;\\nBeyond what can be valu’d, rich or rare;\\nNo less than life, with grace, health, beauty, honour;\\nAs much as child e’er lov’d, or father found;\\nA love that makes breath poor and speech unable;\\nBeyond all manner of so much I love you.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"The focal words directly present Goneril's love for Lear as true. Her ensuing comparisons amplify the asserted degree of that emotional state, making avowal the expression's clear dominant mode, regardless of sincerity.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love you more than word can wield the matter;\\nDearer than eyesight, space, and liberty;\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.9,\"reason\":\"The occurrence reports supposedly extraordinary filial love but does not itself bind Goneril to an action, duty, or continuing relational undertaking. Lear asked which daughter loves him most, and the child-parent comparison characterizes the amount of love rather than affirming an obligation. Lear's subsequent grant is his response to the declaration, not uptake of a commitment made by Goneril.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\\nThat we our largest bounty may extend\\nWhere nature doth with merit challenge.—Goneril,\\nOur eldest born, speak first.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"As much as child e’er lov’d, or father found;\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"We make thee lady: to thine and Albany’s issue\\nBe this perpetual.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.95,\"reason\":\"The expression is part of an elaborate, rhetorically ordered answer to Lear's formal request, not a reflex-like affective outburst. Emotional language and hyperbole do not by themselves establish expressive discharge.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\\nDearer than eyesight, space, and liberty;\\nBeyond what can be valu’d, rich or rare;\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"A love that makes breath poor and speech unable;\\nBeyond all manner of so much I love you.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.82,\"reason\":\"Beyond avowing love, the expression functions as competitive ceremonial praise and a claim to merit Lear's bounty. This strategic, encomiastic function is moderately supported by Lear's explicit invitation to compare the daughters' love in connection with their shares of the kingdom.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\\nThat we our largest bounty may extend\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Beyond what can be valu’d, rich or rare;\\nNo less than life, with grace, health, beauty, honour;\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"In a public division of his kingdom, Lear asks his daughters to say which loves him most so that he may extend his largest bounty accordingly. Goneril answers first with the focal avowal and an extended series of superlative comparisons.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"unknown\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"No undertaking by Goneril is established for this occurrence. Lear accepts the speech as satisfying his requested declaration and rewards it with territory, but that is uptake of a profession of love and competitive praise, not acceptance of a promise or obligation. The supplied evidence does not settle Goneril's sincerity.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied text is recognisable as William Shakespeare's The Tragedy of King Lear, Act I, Scene I. The assessment uses only the supplied passages.\",\"limitations\":\"The passage clearly establishes the public avowal and its immediate reward, but it does not independently settle Goneril's actual affection, private intention, or later conduct. Cordelia's asides provide skeptical contrast without conclusively proving that Goneril's avowal is false.\"}"
